@flexiarg institutions/resource-boundaries
@title Resource Boundaries
@keywords boundaries, resource system, extent, Ostrom 1B, common-pool resources
@audience institution designers, commons researchers
@tone normative
@style pattern
@grade principle
@provenance Ostrom design principle 1B, wording as in Cox, Arnold and Tomas (2010), "A review of design principles for community-based natural resource management", Ecology and Society 15(4):38, quoted in Corneli 2016 (ICCC 2016) Table 1. Original source Ostrom 1990, Governing the Commons. Drafted by claude-16 with Joe, 2026-10-29.
@status draft 2026-10-29; to vet against Ostrom 1990 and Cox et al. 2010 before relying on non-quoted text
@references [institutions/ARGUMENT creativity-commons/place-boundaries]

! conclusion:
  "Clear boundaries are present that define a resource system and separate it from the larger biophysical environment."

  + context:
    Users can only manage what they can delimit: which stretch of river, which forest, which stock.

  + IF:
    A resource system is embedded in a larger environment that affects it.

  + HOWEVER:
    Some resources move or fluctuate (fish stocks, groundwater), so a boundary drawn on a map does not contain them.

  + THEN:
    Define the extent of the resource system that the rules govern, and record it where users can see it.

  + BECAUSE:
    Rules about use presuppose an agreed object of use; disputes about extent otherwise become disputes about everything.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Vet the HOWEVER and BECAUSE against Ostrom 1990 and Cox et al. 2010; only the conclusion is quoted.]
    next[Creativity-commons transposition: creativity-commons/place-boundaries.]
